    dtps: "1. New diagnosis of POAG with moderate field loss — needs consistent treatment to prevent progression. 2. Requires long-term monitoring and medication adherence.",
    monitoring: "IOP measurement at each visit. Visual field testing every 6-12 months. Optic disc photography/OCT annually. Check adherence and technique at each visit. Monitor for side effects: Latanoprost (conjunctival hyperaemia, increased iris pigmentation, eyelash growth), Timolol (bradycardia, bronchospasm — caution in asthma/COPD).",
    counselling: "Glaucoma is a lifelong condition. The drops do not cure glaucoma — they control it. You must take them every day even if your vision feels fine. Most people with glaucoma never go blind if they take treatment. The drops work best when taken consistently. Once vision is lost, it cannot be restored — the goal is to prevent further loss.",
    followUp: "Ophthalmology clinic: 4-6 weeks for IOP check, then every 3-12 months depending on control. If progression despite medical therapy: refer for SLT or trabeculectomy. Annual visual fields. Inform family members to get screened for glaucoma.",
    pearls: "Glaucoma is the leading cause of irreversible blindness worldwide. POAG is asymptomatic until late stages — by the time patients notice vision loss, significant damage has occurred. The ONLY modifiable risk factor is IOP. A single missed dose of Latanoprost can cause IOP to rise within 24 hours. Punctal occlusion after drop instillation reduces systemic absorption by 60-70%. Prostaglandin analogues are first-line because of superior IOP-lowering efficacy, once-daily dosing, and minimal systemic side effects. Beta-blockers (Timolol) must be used with caution in patients with asthma, COPD, or bradycardia.",
