    pharm: "1. Topical antibiotic (first-line): Chloramphenicol 0.5% eye drops — 1-2 drops q2h for 2 days, then q4h for 5-7 days. OR Fusidic Acid 1% gel — BID for 7 days (less frequent, cream-based — good for children). 2. Alternative (if allergy or MRSA): Ciprofloxacin 0.3% or Ofloxacin 0.3% eye drops q2-6h. 3. Cleaning: Gently cleanse eyelids with sterile saline or clean water. 4. No role for systemic antibiotics in simple conjunctivitis.",
    nonPharm: "Hand hygiene — wash hands before and after touching eyes. Do not share towels, pillows, or eye drops. Stay home from school until 24 hours after starting treatment. Dispose of eye makeup. Do not patch the eye (can worsen infection).",
    carePlan: "Start Chloramphenicol 0.5% eye drops q2h for 2 days then q4h. Educate on hygiene and contagion. Review in 3 days if not improving. If worsening (pain, photophobia, decreased vision) — refer to ophthalmology.",
    dtps: "1. Common infectious conjunctivitis in school-aged child with close contact history. 2. Self-limiting but antibiotics reduce duration and prevent spread.",
    monitoring: "Symptoms daily (pain, redness, discharge). If not improved by 72 hours: consider alternative diagnosis, culture, or refer. Contact lens wearers require urgent ophthalmology review (risk of Pseudomonas keratitis).",
    counselling: "Pink eye is highly contagious. Wash your hands often and do not touch or rub your eyes. Use a clean towel each day and do not share with family members. The drops go into the eye — do not touch the tip to the eye or eyelashes. You can return to school 24 hours after starting treatment.",
    followUp: "Follow-up only if not improving in 3 days. If recurrent: consider nasolacrimal duct obstruction (irrigate) or chronic infection (culture).",
    pearls: "Simple bacterial conjunctivitis is often self-limiting (7-10 days) but topical antibiotics reduce duration to 2-5 days and reduce contagion. Chloramphenicol is widely used in Kenya and is effective against most gram-positive pathogens. However, Fusidic Acid has better compliance (BID vs frequent drops). The main differential is viral conjunctivitis — more watery discharge, preauricular lymphadenopathy, and often follows an upper respiratory infection. Contact lens wearers are at high risk for Pseudomonas keratitis — a vision-threatening emergency. ALWAYS check for corneal involvement (fluorescein) if the patient reports pain or photophobia.",
